A1. Somebody's girl friend. ~ KATE
A4. What the young folks used to play in the evenings. ~ GAMES
A8. Something milliners do to hats and husbands. ~ TRIM
A12. The only part of a drink you can hear coming. ~ ICE
A14. An expensive kind of coat that New Yorkers go to Palm Beach for. ~ TAN
A16. Mohammed's adopted contribution to crossword puzzles. ~ ALI
A17. Letter carriers. ~ STAMPS
A19. A specimen. ~ SAMPLE
A21. The touring motorist's stand-by. ~ MAP
A22. That bird which is gone but not forgotten. ~ ROC
A24. What we generally run out of when we run out of the city in our car. ~ GAS
A26. Dora thinks that people do this at sleighing parties. ~ SLAUGHTER
A28. What Mayor Walker did when he made his last speech in Albany. ~ SPOKE
A30. Something morons do after they are once divorced. ~ REWED
A33. Things that waiters wait for. ~ TIPS
A34. The other river in Italy. ~ FIUME
A36. So's your old man. ~ SIRE
A37. Our present share of the verb "to be." ~ ARE
A38. A Scotsman thinks this fool thing's a hat. ~ TAM
A39. He looks like Mike. ~ IKE
A40. The flapper's throne. ~ LAP
A41. What a really good rare steak is. ~ RARE
A43. Torment. You know, put soot in her face powder, and that sort of thing. ~ TEASE
A44. The sort of a lady singer who never gets up in the air. ~ ALTO
A45. The language of flappers and cake-eaters; and it's the cat's! ~ SLANG
A47. Insertion. ~ INSET
A48. Doing this is forbidden by the Constitution, but most after-dinner speakers do it any way. ~ TORTURING
A51. What puzzlers insist on calling "a drink"! ~ TEA
A52. Something a pig has but rarely in its eye. ~ STY
A53. This used to rule the waves. ~ NET
A56. Take-offs—along with pants and collars. ~ SHIRTS
A58. What the detective of fiction is. ~ FERRET
A60. What we are always vastly above—in golfing. ~ PAR
A61. This seems to light only on kings. ~ ACE
A63. When it's sufficiently in the plural, they have it. ~ AYE
A64. Something we miss most in going upstairs after a night with the boys. ~ STEP
A65. Take a good, long look! ~ STARE
A66. It sounds deadly, but many a girl does it for a man. ~ DYES

D1. Rhymes with bliss, and is usually synonymous. ~ KISS
D2. What actors should do, and usually don't. ~ ACT
D3. Two of a kind. ~ TEAM
D5. Near. (Has no reference to beer, however.) ~ AT
D6. A river in Quebec, Canada. ~ MAGOG
D7. 50 per cent. of that printer's measure. ~ EN
D9. What the non-alcoholic spirit does for us. ~ RAPS
D10. What a man is (besides a boob) when he calls a doctor. ~ ILL
D11. Manner, carriage, deportment, or what have you? ~ MIEN
D13. The only fruit, besides bananas, that is responsible for the fall of man. ~ APPLE
D15. A drink; and a darned good one. ~ LAGER
D18. Things that burglars hide behind. ~ MASKS
D20. Animals that closely resemble the male horse. ~ MARES
D22. About all the education most of us have. ~ RUDIMENTS
D23. What, in this age of doubtful hooch, every boy ought to know. ~ CHEMISTRY
D25. These used to spend their time on the legs of the A. E. F. ~ SPIRALS
D27. A kind of water that people blow about. ~ AERATED
D28. What New Yorkers see when a blackjack hits them. (Not a cop, of course!) ~ STARS
D29. The sort of thing Caruso went in for. ~ OPERA
D31. Women use them. ~ WILLS
D32. About the only place where "beggars can be snoozers." ~ DEPOT
D34. A flapper would rather die, than be this. ~ FAT
D35. Our old crossword friend; to make a little go a long way. ~ EKE
D42. Come in! ~ ENTER
D44. What we usually discover to be "ire." ~ ANGER
D46. Where ewes are concerned, these are butter halves. ~ GOATS
D47. The circle that New York's 400 move in. ~ INNER
D49. A city in New York State. We don't dare kid it. ~ UTICA
D50. The only thing that ever stung Cleopatra (pl.). ~ ASPS
D51. A species of shoe. ~ TIRE
D54. The thing that helps a waiter carry his liquor so well. ~ TRAY
D55. That Indian tribe the puzzles are rapidly making famous. ~ UTES
D57. What a Prohibitionist talks through. ~ HAT
D59. Something a woman shuts when she goes to sleep. (Mouth is a five-letter word.) ~ EYE
D61. Doubles for No. 5. ~ AT
D62. What an embarrassed young man says when he can't say anything. ~ ER


